Give .env files the template's sections, comments and key order, keeping their values.

Each assignment is moved exactly as written; a key the file lacks gets a `# KEY=` placeholder, and keys the template lacks are kept at the end. Prints file names, key names and line numbers, never a value.

Usage: evnx align [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --example <FILE>
          The template whose layout every file follows. Without it, each file follows its own: `.env.<name>.example` for `.env.<name>` when there is one, else `[defaults] example`, else `.env.example`

      --env <FILE>
          A file to align. Repeat for several: every one is checked before any is written. Defaults to `[defaults] env_name`, else `.env`

      --env-name <NAME>
          Align `.env.<NAME>`. Repeatable, and combines with --env

  -n, --dry-run
          Show what would move, what is missing and what is extra; write nothing

      --check
          Report alignment through the exit code; write nothing.
          
            0  every file already has the template's layout (`evnx sync --check`
               is what reports keys with no value)
            1  a file is out of alignment
            2  no verdict — a file is missing, will not parse, or is the
               same file as another one named

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: `pretty` (default) or `json`
          
          [default: pretty]
          [possible values: pretty, json]

      --no-backup
          Keep no `.bak` copy of a file before replacing it.
          
          Without it, each changed file is kept as a private `.bak` (git-ignored, listed by `evnx history`, put back by `evnx undo`).

  -v, --verbose
          Enable verbose output

  -q, --quiet
          Print nothing on success. Findings, warnings, errors and requested data still print

      --no-color
          Disable colored output

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

What it does:
  The template decides headings, comments, blank lines and key order. Each
  file keeps its own assignments, moved byte for byte: values, quoting,
  inline comments and multiline values are never rewritten.
  A key the file lacks gets a # KEY= placeholder: never the template's value,
  and never a blank KEY=, so what the app reads does not change. Keys the
  template lacks are kept, in order, under "Environment-specific settings".

Which template: the one every command pairs with the file. --example, else
.env.<name>.example for .env.<name> when it exists, else [defaults] example,
else .env.example. Files named together may follow different templates.

Comments only the file has are kept: one directly above an assignment moves
with it, one followed by a blank line stays after the assignment above it.
A comment the template also has is the template's. Duplicate keys, a line
that does not parse, mixed line endings, or more copies of a template comment
than the template has stop the run, and nothing is written.

Examples:
  evnx align --env .env.development --env .env.test
  evnx align --example .env.example --env .env --dry-run
  evnx align --env-name production --check

Every file is read and checked before any is written, and one already aligned
is not touched. A file that changes is kept as .bak first, replacing the last
one (--no-backup to skip); a .bak that .gitignore does not cover is refused,
since it would hold the secrets. If a later file fails, the ones already
replaced are put back. Another evnx run writing the same file stops this one.

Exit codes: 0 aligned (or written, or a dry run); 1 --check found a file out
of alignment; 2 could not run (missing file, parse error, same file twice).

Full guide: https://docs.evnx.dev/cli/commands/align
